-The package's browser half registers a dedicated `vision_inspect` Tool row at the same enablement boundary. It uses a compact eye icon and `Look` title instead of the generic Tool call label. The running row deliberately reads `Look ing <elapsed>` with a small visual gap between the title and activity suffix; the settled row reads `Look <duration>`. The row defines no private sweep and receives the one native in-flight sweep owned by the surrounding Tool call seat. The OpenAI-compatible request uses SSE and appends each visual-model text fragment as a replayable `tool/progress` event keyed by the durable call id. As soon as the structured `summary` begins, that visual observation replaces the delegated prompt after the separator; while streaming, the one-line viewport follows the latest line and its newest text, then returns to the summary's first line when settled. The final structured value still lands once in the paired `tool/result`, and the disclosure retains the exact durable input and output. Progress is log-only and never enters model history, so it does not duplicate the result or change the request prefix. When the bridge is disabled, this keyed presentation is absent and the package exposes no visual Tool UI.
+The package's browser half registers a dedicated `vision_inspect` Tool row at the same enablement boundary. It uses a compact eye icon and `Look` title instead of the generic Tool call label. The running row deliberately reads `Look ing <elapsed>` with a small visual gap between the title and activity suffix; the settled row reads `Look <duration>`. Because this keyed view owns its Think-shaped inline duration, it suppresses the generic trailing Tool duration. The row defines no private sweep and receives the one native in-flight sweep owned by the surrounding Tool call seat. The OpenAI-compatible request uses SSE and appends each visual-model text fragment as a replayable `tool/progress` event keyed by the durable call id. As soon as the structured `summary` begins, that visual observation replaces the delegated prompt after the separator; when generation advances into `observations`, the row follows the newest observation text. While streaming, the one-line viewport follows the latest line and its newest text, then returns to the summary's first line when settled. The final structured value still lands once in the paired `tool/result`, and the disclosure retains the exact durable input and output. Progress is log-only and never enters model history, so it does not duplicate the result or change the request prefix. When the bridge is disabled, this keyed presentation is absent and the package exposes no visual Tool UI.
 
